    planNote: 'You know the wheel and the mirror from part 2, The Paper Enigma. Here there are three rotors at once, as in the real machine, so you read them a little differently: page 2 explains why. Each letter takes a dozen or so steps, so work slowly and check.',

    inOver: 'HOW IT WORKS · STEP BY STEP', inTitle: 'What each wheel does',
    in1Title: 'The black wheel: keyboard, lamps and the names of places',
    in1Text: v => `The black wheel is the big wheel from part 2: the keyboard and the lamps. It does not turn, so its letters name the **places**: the 26 narrow wedges from the pin to the edge, the same on every wheel. Place ${v.exPlug} is the wedge with the black ${v.exPlug}. This is where the current comes in from the key and where it goes out to a lamp. It does not change letters (key A leads to place A), which is why it has no coloured strip. Rejewski had to guess this, because the commercial Enigma did it differently.`,
    in2Title: 'A rotor: wires inside',
    in2Text: v => `A real rotor is a thick disc with 26 contacts on each side, in the same places and with the same letters. Inside, 26 wires join a letter on one side to a different letter on the other. On paper both sides are one strip: coloured letters at the edge, black ones inside, and between them a **white path**, the inside of the rotor. Over each black letter there is a coloured one: that is where its wire ends. Over the black {{${v.exWire}}} there is a coloured {{${v.exRead}}}, so the wire runs from {{${v.exWire}}} to {{${v.exRead}}}.`,
    in2Rule: '**On the way in**: the current comes from the edge, so it meets the coloured letter first. Read it → go along the white path to the same black letter → move the pencil there.',
    figReal: 'a real rotor from the side', figPaper: 'the same on paper',
    in3Title: 'The wheels one under another',
    in3Text: v => `Imagine the wheels cut open at the window and straightened into strips, one under another: a place is now a column. On a rotor the current runs sideways along the white path (the arrow), then goes straight down onto the next wheel, **in the same column**. The letter there is different, because that rotor is turned differently, so you always look for the letter on the same wheel. The mirror does not turn and has its wires drawn on it: each joins two letters into a pair. Right after the black wheel comes the plugboard: you read it like a rotor, but it does not turn. The drawing shows the letter from page 3: key {{${v.exKey}}}, lamp {{${v.exLamp}}}.`,
    ladderAria: 'The wheels cut open and straightened, one under another, with the way of the current',
    ladderWin: v => `yellow column: the window ({{${v.win1}}}, read from the bottom)`,
    in4Title: 'The way back',
    in4Text: v => `From the mirror the current goes back by another way, from the centre to the edge, so it meets the black letter first: **read it → go along the white path to the same coloured letter → move the pencil there**. The rule is the same: read the row you come to, look in the other one. Last comes the plugboard: ${v.outCable ? `the coloured {{${v.exBack}}} is over the black {{${v.exLamp}}} (the ${v.exBack}–${v.exLamp} cable), so it is lamp {{${v.exLamp}}}` : `the slot over the black {{${v.exBack}}} is empty, so it is lamp {{${v.exLamp}}}`}.`,
    newTitle: 'What is different from part 2, The Paper Enigma',
    newList: v => [
      `**You turn before the letter, not after it**: in the real Enigma the key first turns the right rotor, and only then does the current flow. With the key {{${v.start}}} the first letter already goes at {{${v.win1}}}.`,
      'Behind the wheel there is no mirror straight away but the next rotor, turned differently. So what counts is the **place** where the current comes out, not the name of the letter, and that is why a rotor has black letters too.',
      'The coloured letters are at the edge and the black ones inside, the other way round from part 2, because the current comes from the edge. So you never turn back on a wheel.',
    ],

    howOver: 'HOW IT WORKS · THE WHOLE MACHINE', howTitle: 'Six wheels on one split pin',
    howIntro: 'The real Enigma had a **plugboard**, **three rotors** and a **mirror**. On paper that is six wheels on one split pin: the black wheel with the keyboard, the plugboard ring, the three rotors and, on top, the small mirror. You only see the edge of each wheel, because a smaller one lies on it.',
    bigAria: 'The paper Enigma put together, and the way of the current',
    legendIn: 'current to the mirror', legendOut: 'current back from the mirror', legendWin: v => `window (yellow): {{${v.win1}}}, read from the centre`,
    exTitle: v => `Example: you press {{${v.exKey}}}, lamp {{${v.exLamp}}} lights up`,
    chainNames: ['key', 'plugboard', 'right', 'middle', 'left', 'mirror', 'left', 'middle', 'right', 'plugboard', 'lamp'],
    frameName: 'black wheel',
    exText: v => `First the right rotor turns by one letter: the window showed {{${v.start}}}, now it shows {{${v.win1}}}. You lay the pencil at key {{${v.exKey}}} on the black wheel. ${v.inCable ? `On the plugboard you meet a coloured {{${v.exPlug}}} there (the ${v.exKey}–${v.exPlug} cable): go along the white path to the black {{${v.exPlug}}}.` : `On the plugboard the slot over {{${v.exKey}}} is empty (no cable), so you go straight down to the black {{${v.exPlug}}}.`} On the right rotor you first meet a coloured {{${v.exRead}}} there: go along the white path to the black {{${v.exRead}}} and move the pencil to it. Now go on to the middle rotor, but at the same place, by the pencil: there is a coloured {{${v.exRead2}}} there, so you look for the black {{${v.exRead2}}} on the middle rotor. And so on, wheel by wheel: the boxes show the letters you read and look for, one after another (circled in the drawing: red on the way to the mirror, blue on the way back). At the end, on the plugboard, at the black {{${v.exBack}}}${v.outCable ? ` you look for the coloured {{${v.exBack}}}: it is over the black {{${v.exLamp}}} (the ${v.exBack}–${v.exLamp} cable), so lamp {{${v.exLamp}}} lights up` : ` the slot is empty, so you go straight on to lamp {{${v.exLamp}}}`}.`,

